Scope every write's `WHERE` clause to `req.user.accountId`, and return 404 when no row matches. Three routes need this. `POST /orders` already does it, so use it as the model.

**`routes/accounts.js`**

```js
router.patch("/:id", async (req, res) => {
  if (req.params.id !== String(req.user.accountId)) return res.status(404).end();
  const row = await db.write(
    "UPDATE accounts SET display_name = $1, billing_email = $2 WHERE id = $3 RETURNING *",
    [req.body.display_name, req.body.billing_email, req.user.accountId]
  );
  if (!row) return res.status(404).end();
  res.json(row);
});

router.delete("/:id", async (req, res) => {
  if (req.params.id !== String(req.user.accountId)) return res.status(404).end();
  const row = await db.write("UPDATE accounts SET deleted_at = now() WHERE id = $1 RETURNING id",
    [req.user.accountId]);
  if (!row) return res.status(404).end();
  res.status(204).end();
});
```

**`routes/orders.js`**

```js
router.patch("/:id/cancel", async (req, res) => {
  const row = await db.write(
    "UPDATE orders SET status = 'cancelled' WHERE id = $1 AND account_id = $2 RETURNING *",
    [req.params.id, req.user.accountId]
  );
  if (!row) return res.status(404).end();
  res.json(row);
});
```

Notes:

- The `:id` check in the accounts routes is required. Without it, `PATCH /accounts/<other id>` would silently update your own account, and `DELETE` would soft-delete it. The `String()` is there because `req.params.id` is always a string, while pg may return the account id as a number.
- The `if (!row)` checks matter. `write()` returns `rows[0]`, so a non-match is `undefined`, and `res.json(undefined)` currently sends a 200 with an empty body.
- Whether any user should be able to soft-delete the whole account is a separate question from scoping. The schema visible here has no role column, so there is no owner check to add. If an owner or admin role exists elsewhere, restrict the `DELETE` to it.
- The tree has no tests. Add one per route that logs in as account A and expects a 404 for account B's id.

Separately, nothing in these files sets `req.cookies`. `app.js` loads no cookie parser, so `sessionUser` queries with a NULL token and every request gets a 401. Nothing can be exercised end to end until that is wired up. There is no `package.json` in the tree, so I can't confirm whether a cookie parser is loaded somewhere I haven't seen.

Add one middleware in `app.js`. That covers both route files without touching them.

```js
// Logs one JSON line per response. Registered before sessionUser so 401s are logged too.
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  const start = process.hrtime.bigint();
  res.on("finish", () => {
    console.log(JSON.stringify({
      method: req.method,
      path: req.path,
      status: res.statusCode,
      ms: Number(process.hrtime.bigint() - start) / 1e6,
      userId: req.user && req.user.id,
      accountId: req.user && req.user.accountId,
    }));
  });
  next();
});
```

`req.user` is read at `finish`, when `sessionUser` has already set it on authenticated requests. Unauthenticated requests log with empty user fields.

Leave out request bodies and headers. The PATCH body carries `billing_email`, and the headers carry the session cookie.

A request that fails through the unhandled rejection described earlier never emits `finish`, so it gets no line. Fixing that needs an error handler, which the crash path also needs.

This writes to stdout with no logger library, which is enough if your host collects stdout. Add a library only when you need log levels or shipping.

