Capture every write with a Postgres trigger on `accounts` and `orders`, and have `db.write()` tell the database who the actor is, inside the same transaction. The app can't reliably record the change itself, because `write()` is only a convention: `query()` can run an UPDATE, and a future route or script can skip the helper. A trigger fires on every write regardless of path, which is what "every write" requires. `write()` still has to supply the actor, since the trigger can't see the session.

**1. Pass the actor into `write()` and set it for the transaction only**

```js
async function write(actorId, text, params) {
  if (!actorId) throw new Error("write() requires an actor");
  const client = await pool.connect();
  try {
    await client.query("BEGIN");
    await client.query("SELECT set_config('app.actor_id', $1, true)", [actorId]);
    const res = await client.query(text, params);
    await client.query("COMMIT");
    return res.rows[0];
  } catch (err) {
    await client.query("ROLLBACK");
    throw err;
  } finally {
    client.release();
  }
}
```

The `true` makes the setting last only for the transaction. Without it, the value stays on the pooled connection and the next request inherits it. The four call sites become `db.write(req.user.id, "UPDATE accounts ...", [...])` and so on, in `routes/accounts.js` and `routes/orders.js`.

**2. Add the audit table and triggers**

```sql
CREATE TABLE audit_log (
  id          bigserial PRIMARY KEY,
  occurred_at timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),
  table_name  text NOT NULL,
  row_id      text NOT NULL,
  action      text NOT NULL,
  actor_id    text NOT NULL,
  before      jsonb,
  after       jsonb
);

CREATE FUNCTION audit_row() RETURNS trigger
LANGUAGE plpgsql AS $$
DECLARE
  actor text := NULLIF(current_setting('app.actor_id', true), '');
BEGIN
  IF actor IS NULL THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'write to % has no actor set', TG_TABLE_NAME;
  END IF;
  INSERT INTO audit_log (table_name, row_id, action, actor_id, before, after)
  VALUES (
    TG_TABLE_NAME,
    COALESCE(NEW.id, OLD.id)::text,
    TG_OP,
    actor,
    CASE WHEN TG_OP <> 'INSERT' THEN to_jsonb(OLD) END,
    CASE WHEN TG_OP <> 'DELETE' THEN to_jsonb(NEW) END
  );
  RETURN NULL;
END;
$$;

CREATE TRIGGER accounts_audit AFTER INSERT OR UPDATE OR DELETE ON accounts
  FOR EACH ROW EXECUTE FUNCTION audit_row();
CREATE TRIGGER orders_audit AFTER INSERT OR UPDATE OR DELETE ON orders
  FOR EACH ROW EXECUTE FUNCTION audit_row();
```

Storing before and after row images means the previous status on cancel and the `deleted_at` change on soft delete are captured without extra reads in app code. The audit insert commits or rolls back with the change, because both run in `write()`'s transaction.

**3. Lock the log down**

```sql
REVOKE UPDATE, DELETE, TRUNCATE ON audit_log FROM app_role;
GRANT INSERT, SELECT ON audit_log TO app_role;
GRANT USAGE ON SEQUENCE audit_log_id_seq TO app_role;
```

The app role must not own `accounts`, `orders`, or `audit_log`. An owner can disable the trigger or edit the log.

