Capture the changes in the database with a trigger on each written table, and pass the actor into `write()`. `write()` is already the one transaction boundary, but nothing forces a write through it (`query()` will run an UPDATE just as well), so application-level logging would miss writes. A trigger records every row change, whatever path made it.

**1. Pass the actor through `write()`.** `set_config(..., true)` lasts only until commit or rollback, so a pooled connection can't carry one request's actor into the next.

```js
async function write(actor, text, params) {
  const client = await pool.connect();
  try {
    await client.query("BEGIN");
    await client.query("SELECT set_config('app.actor_id', $1, true)", [actor]);
    const res = await client.query(text, params);
    await client.query("COMMIT");
    return res.rows[0];
  } catch (err) {
    await client.query("ROLLBACK");
    throw err;
  } finally {
    client.release();
  }
}
```

Each call site becomes `db.write(req.user.id, ...)`: `routes/accounts.js` lines 7 and 15, `routes/orders.js` lines 7 and 15.

**2. Add the audit table and triggers.** Each row records the actor, table, row id, operation, and the full row before and after. The trigger raises if no actor is set, so a write that skips `write()` fails instead of landing unattributed. System jobs pass an explicit actor such as `system:<job>`.

```sql
CREATE TABLE audit_log (
  id          bigserial   PRIMARY KEY,
  occurred_at timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT clock_timestamp(),
  actor       text        NOT NULL,
  table_name  text        NOT NULL,
  row_id      text        NOT NULL,
  op          text        NOT NULL,
  before      jsonb,
  after       jsonb
);

CREATE FUNCTION audit_row() RETURNS trigger LANGUAGE plpgsql AS $$
DECLARE
  actor_id text := current_setting('app.actor_id', true);
BEGIN
  IF actor_id IS NULL OR actor_id = '' THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'write to % has no actor; call db.write(actor, ...)', TG_TABLE_NAME;
  END IF;
  INSERT INTO audit_log (actor, table_name, row_id, op, before, after)
  VALUES (
    actor_id,
    TG_TABLE_NAME,
    COALESCE(to_jsonb(NEW) ->> 'id', to_jsonb(OLD) ->> 'id'),
    TG_OP,
    CASE WHEN TG_OP <> 'INSERT' THEN to_jsonb(OLD) END,
    CASE WHEN TG_OP <> 'DELETE' THEN to_jsonb(NEW) END
  );
  RETURN NULL;
END $$;

CREATE TRIGGER audit_accounts AFTER INSERT OR UPDATE OR DELETE ON accounts
  FOR EACH ROW EXECUTE FUNCTION audit_row();
CREATE TRIGGER audit_orders AFTER INSERT OR UPDATE OR DELETE ON orders
  FOR EACH ROW EXECUTE FUNCTION audit_row();
```

**3. Make the log append-only.** The app role gets `INSERT` and `SELECT` on `audit_log` and nothing else. Revoke `TRUNCATE` on the audited tables too, since row triggers don't fire on it. Table ownership should sit with a migration role, not the app role, so the app can't run `ALTER TABLE ... DISABLE TRIGGER`.

